00:04:22.280 --> 00:04:29.680
But first, from Wikipedia, John Kiriakou is an associate fellow with the Institute

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:35.120
for Policy Studies and a blogger for Huffington Post.

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:42.320
He was a CIA analyst and case officer, senior investigator for the Senate Foreign Relations

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:51.080
Committee, and counterterrorism consultant for ABC News and an author of several books.

00:04:51.080 --> 00:04:59.100
He was the first U.S. government official to confirm in December 2007 that waterboarding

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:06.780
was used to interrogate al-Qaeda prisoners, and he described that as torture.

00:05:06.780 --> 00:05:15.220
On October 22, 2012, Kiriyaku pleaded guilty to disclosing classified information about

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:23.540
a fellow CIA officer that connected the covert operative to a specific operation.

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:32.140
He was sentenced to 30 months in prison on January 25, 2013 and served his term from

00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:44.060
February 28, 2013 until February 3, 2015 at the Low Security Federal Correction Facility

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:47.100
in Loretto, Pennsylvania.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:51.660
I called him at his home in Arlington, Virginia.

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:53.380
Give a listen.

00:05:53.380 --> 00:05:57.020
John Kiriaku, welcome to tell somebody.

00:05:57.020 --> 00:05:58.740
Thank you so much for having me.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.180
Very glad you're able to take the time.

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:02.300
We appreciate it.

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:04.020
So you've been home for a while.

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:09.020
You were in prison and then under house arrest.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:13.660
But you're totally free now, is that right?

00:06:13.660 --> 00:06:15.340
For the most part, free.

00:06:15.340 --> 00:06:17.580
I have three years of probation.

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:21.340
So from May 1st until July 1st,

00:06:21.340 --> 00:06:27.020
I'm under a travel ban, meaning I'm not allowed to leave the Washington, D.C. area.

00:06:27.020 --> 00:06:35.260
And then from July 1st until May 1st of 2018, I have to get permission to travel outside

00:06:35.260 --> 00:06:37.900
the Washington area two weeks in advance.

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:42.260
And if I need to travel internationally for any reason, I have to go before a judge

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:44.060
and ask for permission.

00:06:44.060 --> 00:06:50.420
But this is, I was reading about your house arrest conditions, and I'm sure it was

00:06:50.420 --> 00:06:58.420
It was great to get out of actual full incarceration, but these seem like pretty harsh methods.

00:06:58.420 --> 00:07:01.420
Can you talk about what kind of restrictions you were on there?

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:06.420
Frankly, I was surprised at how difficult house arrest was.

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:11.420
I just figured house arrest, I'm going to be with my wife and kids again.

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:13.420
It's going to be fantastic.

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:17.420
But the truth of the matter is house arrest means literally that.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:22.940
are confined to the four walls of your house and you can't leave for any reason except to

00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:29.060
go to the doctor to go to work or to go to the halfway house to which you're assigned.

00:07:29.060 --> 00:07:35.860
And I worked from home, so I literally had no reason to leave the house except to go

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:42.940
to the halfway house to check in and be drug tested on Tuesdays and Thursdays.

00:07:42.940 --> 00:07:50.480
And if I remember right, it seems like it took a couple hours, three, four hours out of just

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:51.980
about every day to do this.

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:52.980
Yeah.

00:07:52.980 --> 00:07:53.980
Yeah.

00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:54.980
At first I had to go to the halfway house every day.

00:07:54.980 --> 00:08:01.700
And of course, like most halfway houses, it's in the worst part of town.

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:03.900
I was not allowed to drive.

00:08:03.900 --> 00:08:08.980
And so I had to walk to the subway, take the subway to Capitol Hill, then get a

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:16.980
bus the rest of the way into southeast Washington and then walk from the bus station to the halfway

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:17.980
house.

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:22.980
So it took me two hours to get there, two hours to get home, plus whatever amount of

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:26.500
time I was in the halfway house, which was usually another hour or two.

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:31.420
And this was in the middle of the day, so these officials at the halfway house are

00:08:31.420 --> 00:08:37.140
yelling at me every day to get a job, get a job, get a job, but I'm spending six

00:08:37.140 --> 00:08:43.060
hours in the middle of the workday just getting up there to see them and getting back again.

00:08:43.060 --> 00:08:45.600
It made it almost impossible to find work.

00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:50.580
This is just almost Kafkaesque weird, it seems to me.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:51.580
That's what I called it.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:57.580
I called it Kafkaesque because it says though the entire system is trying to force you to

00:08:57.580 --> 00:08:58.580
fail.

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:00.620
It's like they don't want you to succeed.

00:09:00.620 --> 00:09:05.140
They don't want you to find another job and they're looking for a reason to violate

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:07.780
you and send you back to prison.

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:15.060
And how typical is this kind of craziness for people getting out of prison generally?

00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:20.620
Or do a lot of people with maybe less ability to deal with it than you had have to deal with

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:21.620
this kind of thing?

00:09:21.620 --> 00:09:25.740
That's a very important question and the answer is a resounding yes.

00:09:25.740 --> 00:09:33.260
I'm very fortunate because I have a master's degree and I have extensive work experience.

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:37.260
I have areas of expertise and I have a lot of friends.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:44.860
But if you're poor, if you're a minority, if you have no education and no job skills,

00:09:44.860 --> 00:09:51.180
and you're expected to get not just a job, but a good job, a real job, a job with which

00:09:51.180 --> 00:09:56.500
you can support your family, as soon as you get out of prison, you're in for a

00:09:56.500 --> 00:09:57.500
rude awakening.

00:09:57.500 --> 00:10:04.600
Part of the problem is that prisons in our country reform no one, they rehabilitate no

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:06.700
one and they train no one.

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:12.780
There are no vocational education courses, there are no college courses anymore, there's

00:10:12.780 --> 00:10:15.780
nothing except a GED class.

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:20.260
And if you already have a GED, you get nothing else.

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:21.260
That's it.

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:26.300
So if you went to prison and the only thing you knew how to do was to sell drugs,

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:29.020
chances are pretty good that you're going to sell drugs when you get out of

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:33.260
prison. You got to go with what you know. You got to go with what you know and

00:10:33.260 --> 00:10:37.760
they're always on your back about being able to provide for your family and for

00:10:37.760 --> 00:10:43.980
a lot of guys who know nothing else and and really have no job skills who do

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:47.220
want to provide for their families the only thing they can do is to sell

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:55.100
drugs. Well we could do a whole show or two on that and we do have some

00:10:55.100 --> 00:11:00.060
shows on this station that do that, but I'm wreaking havoc with any kind of

00:11:00.060 --> 00:11:07.460
chronological telling of your story, but before we get to some of the CIA stuff,

00:11:07.460 --> 00:11:13.580
can you briefly tell me what what the actual in prison part of your

00:11:13.580 --> 00:11:19.100
experience was like? Yeah, I thought that I was going to go to a minimum

00:11:19.100 --> 00:11:26.100
security federal work camp the judge had recommended that the prosecution had

00:11:26.100 --> 00:11:33.500
agreed to it and so when I when I turned myself in at the at the prison at

00:11:33.500 --> 00:11:39.780
Loretto Pennsylvania February 28th 2013 I just assumed I was going to the camp

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:44.740
because that's what everybody had told me in fact I was escorted to the back

00:11:44.740 --> 00:11:51.520
of the prison and taken into the actual prison itself and when I said something to the corrections

00:11:51.520 --> 00:11:55.060
officer who was leading the way I said hey wait a minute I'm supposed to be at the camp

00:11:55.060 --> 00:12:01.220
across the street and he said not according to my paperwork you're not and so I went to

00:12:01.220 --> 00:12:07.980
the actual prison so I figured there was no sense in complaining I had a relatively

00:12:07.980 --> 00:12:14.700
short sentence if I even filed a motion with the court it would probably take years

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:20.660
to get a hearing so I just toughed it out but about halfway through my stay in prison

00:12:20.660 --> 00:12:26.460
I filed a Freedom of Information Act request to see if I could determine why they had pulled

00:12:26.460 --> 00:12:33.500
a fast one like this and sent me to prison rather than to the work camp and I got 250

00:12:33.500 --> 00:12:41.540
pages of nonsense but tucked in those 250 pages were 6 or 7 or 8 pages marked FOIA

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:47.380
exempt do not release to inmate and they were accidentally included in this

00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:55.060
package that I received and what they said in big bold large type letters it

00:12:55.060 --> 00:13:01.820
said caution inmate has access to the press and it was in those documents that

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:11.380
they decided to classify me or categorize me as what they called critical

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:17.600
threat the highest security level wow and that meant that I could not be outside

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:23.680
the double the double fences I had to be inside that barbed wire greatest

00:13:23.680 --> 00:13:28.360
severity is what it was called greatest severity and it wasn't because I was

00:13:28.360 --> 00:13:32.480
dangerous it wasn't because I was trained by the CIA to escape from places

00:13:32.480 --> 00:13:39.160
like this I wasn't the sole reason was that I had access to the press yeah

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:45.880
Yeah, that is the greatest fear of a lot of these folks, I'm afraid, isn't it?

00:13:45.880 --> 00:13:46.880
It is.

00:13:46.880 --> 00:13:47.880
It is.

00:13:47.880 --> 00:13:48.880
And there's no oversight.

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:55.160
I mean, these Bureau of Prisons people can literally do anything they want to anybody

00:13:55.160 --> 00:14:00.120
and you have no recourse.

00:14:00.120 --> 00:14:07.280
So many things I want to ask you, but given the shortness of time, I want to make sure

00:14:07.280 --> 00:14:13.960
we get what you did, what they say you did, what you were convicted of.

00:14:13.960 --> 00:14:22.320
Can you find a starting point for that tale and what were the charges and what's your

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:26.360
take on what you did and so forth?

00:14:26.360 --> 00:14:27.360
Sure.

00:14:27.360 --> 00:14:35.640
My life changed completely on December 11th, 2007.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:43.120
I went on ABC News on that day and I said in a nationally televised broadcast that the CIA

00:14:43.120 --> 00:14:50.000
was torturing prisoners, that torture was official U.S. government policy, and that

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:52.840
the policy had been approved by the president himself.

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:59.240
Now, the president had said just days before that we were not torturing prisoners.

00:14:59.240 --> 00:15:02.920
And he looked right in the camera and he said, we do not torture.

00:15:02.920 --> 00:15:05.560
And I knew that that was a lie.

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:11.920
And then later he said that if there was torture it was a result of rogue interrogators, rogue

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:12.920
CIA officers.

00:15:12.920 --> 00:15:16.040
Well I knew that was also a lie.

00:15:16.040 --> 00:15:24.400
And so in 2007 I decided to come forward and to tell the truth about torture.

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:35.400
I've been convinced ever since then that I was prosecuted because of that interview.

00:15:35.400 --> 00:15:41.640
Now I ended up taking a plea to a lesser charge, a charge of violating the Intelligence Identities

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:44.000
Protection Act of 1982.

00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:50.320
I confirmed the name of a former colleague to a journalist who never published it.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:54.160
And for that I was charged with a felony to which I plead guilty.

00:15:54.160 --> 00:15:59.880
Now I'm convinced that that was just, you know, they got me on whatever they could

00:15:59.880 --> 00:16:00.960
get me.

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:03.040
People leak in Washington every day.

00:16:03.040 --> 00:16:10.920
The last three CIA directors released the names of covert operatives, most famously General

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:16.520
Petraeus, who released the names of dozens of officers to his girlfriend.

00:16:16.520 --> 00:16:20.800
But there were no prosecutions for two of those three CIA directors, and General Petraeus

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:25.600
got a sweetheart deal in which he agreed to plead guilty to a misdemeanor, no jail

00:16:25.600 --> 00:16:35.080
time, $100,000 fine and 18 months of unsupervised parole or probation, I didn't get that sweetheart

00:16:35.080 --> 00:16:36.080
deal.

00:16:36.080 --> 00:16:39.800
Neither did any of the other whistleblowers that have come up on espionage charges since

00:16:39.800 --> 00:16:44.280
Tom Drake at the tail end of the Bush administration.

00:16:44.280 --> 00:16:48.400
And $100,000 fine that Trace had to pay.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:50.000
Yes, the quarter of his normal speaking fees.

00:16:50.000 --> 00:16:53.800
Yeah, I was just going to say that would be a lot of money for a lot of us, including

00:16:53.800 --> 00:16:54.800
me.

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:57.800
He's just donated part of one of his speeches.

00:16:57.800 --> 00:17:00.800
But apparently, General Petraeus makes that in 45 minutes.

00:17:00.800 --> 00:17:02.800
I don't think he'll have any problem.

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:03.800
That's amazing.

00:17:03.800 --> 00:17:07.800
I was going to ask you what you thought of his sentence

00:17:07.800 --> 00:17:09.800
and compare it to yours,

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:13.800
and then what was this latest one, Sterling?

00:17:13.800 --> 00:17:15.800
Yeah, Jeffrey Sterling.

00:17:15.800 --> 00:17:17.800
Well, regarding General Petraeus,

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:22.800
as much as it causes a knot in my stomach to say it,

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:27.160
I really don't believe that General Petraeus should have been charged with a crime.

00:17:27.160 --> 00:17:30.240
For the same reason that I shouldn't have been charged with a crime.

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:32.960
First of all, neither one of us had any criminal intent.

00:17:32.960 --> 00:17:37.680
And secondly, neither one of us caused any harm to the national security.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:41.760
But General Petraeus is friends with the president, and so he got a sweetheart deal.

00:17:41.760 --> 00:17:44.280
I didn't get a sweetheart deal, and neither did Jeffrey Sterling.

00:17:44.280 --> 00:17:50.360
Now, Jeffrey Sterling was a CIA case officer, an operations officer, who was accused

00:17:50.360 --> 00:17:55.200
of providing information about a botched CIA operation

00:17:55.200 --> 00:18:00.560
to provide flawed information to Iran's nuclear program

00:18:00.560 --> 00:18:03.960
by using a Russian scientist.

00:18:03.960 --> 00:18:06.920
Sterling complained about this botched operation

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:10.600
to the House Permanent Select Committee on Intelligence.

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:14.320
For whatever reason, the CIA got it into their heads

00:18:14.320 --> 00:18:17.720
that Sterling was the source of a book written

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:23.620
by the New York Times reporter James Ryzen, called State of War in 2006.

00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:30.800
Ryzen has never said that Sterling was the source, and Sterling has always maintained

00:18:30.800 --> 00:18:32.280
that he was not the source.

00:18:32.280 --> 00:18:42.200
And indeed, at his trial, Ryzen would say only that he had many sources for that story.

00:18:42.200 --> 00:18:46.960
But without any direct evidence, there were no emails, there was no smoking gun, there

00:18:46.960 --> 00:18:53.960
there were no recorded conversations, nothing. Just metadata indicating that emails had been

00:18:53.960 --> 00:19:01.520
exchanged between Ryzen and Sterling. Sterling was found guilty on nine felony counts. That

00:19:01.520 --> 00:19:07.520
is seven counts of espionage and two counts of theft of government property, with the

00:19:07.520 --> 00:19:14.920
property being the information. Now, Sterling was facing life in prison because of this

00:19:14.920 --> 00:19:20.240
verdict, which I thought was just insane. There was literally no evidence to convict him, but

00:19:20.240 --> 00:19:27.720
in the Eastern District of Virginia, where both Sterling and I had our cases, it's called

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:31.360
the Espionage Court because anyone charged with a national security crime is going to

00:19:31.360 --> 00:19:35.480
go to prison, whether he did it or not. That's just the reputation of the court and of

00:19:35.480 --> 00:19:42.480
the juries that appear in that court. So Sterling was looking at life in prison

00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:46.760
and his attorney told him you know

00:19:46.760 --> 00:19:50.320
something on the lines of twenty five years

00:19:50.320 --> 00:19:54.920
the prosecution recommended a sentence of nineteen to twenty four years

00:19:54.920 --> 00:19:57.920
certainly judge who happened to be the same judge that i had

00:19:57.920 --> 00:19:59.680
judge leonnie brinkama

00:19:59.680 --> 00:20:03.320
uh... you know just brinkama read the newspapers to which he thought that

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:04.640
sweetheart deal

00:20:04.640 --> 00:20:06.920
that tray scott

00:20:06.920 --> 00:20:10.400
the district of virginia while it's a tough district

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:12.720
it doesn't operate in a vacuum

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:15.440
and so instead of the nineteen to twenty four

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:16.560
years

00:20:16.560 --> 00:20:18.960
uh... she ended up giving sterling

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:20.800
three-and-a-half years

00:20:20.800 --> 00:20:25.440
now even three-and-a-half years is too much because like i said i think the guy

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:26.720
didn't commit the crime

00:20:26.720 --> 00:20:28.320
and i think he certainly has

00:20:28.320 --> 00:20:31.600
appealable issues but he'll have to appeal those issues from

00:20:31.600 --> 00:20:34.160
a prison cell

